   "text": "This is angiotensin II (Asp\u2011Arg\u2011Val\u2011Tyr\u2011Ile\u2011His\u2011Pro\u2011Phe), drawn as a linear octapeptide from the N\u2011terminal Asp to the C\u2011terminal Phe.\n\n**Residues, N\u2011 to C\u2011terminus:**\n- Asp has a free NH2 and a CH2COOH side chain.\n- Arg has a propyl chain ending in a guanidine drawn as N=C(NH2)NH2.\n- Val has an isopropyl side chain.\n- Tyr has a 4\u2011hydroxybenzyl side chain.\n- Ile has a sec\u2011butyl side chain.\n- His has an imidazol\u20114\u2011ylmethyl side chain.\n- Pro is the five\u2011membered ring that links His and Phe.\n- Phe has a benzyl side chain and a free COOH.\n\n**Stereochemistry:** I assigned each wedge or hash centre by its drawn geometry. All eight \u03b1\u2011carbons are S, which is the natural L configuration. The Ile \u03b2\u2011carbon (hashed methyl) is also S, giving the usual (2S,3S) isoleucine.\n\nI then converted this to SMILES and checked each centre's @/@@ against the standard L\u2011amino\u2011acid patterns, which required correcting Val and Ile from my first draft.\n\nSMILES: N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCN=C(N)N)C(=O)N[C@@H](C(C)C)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccc(O)cc1)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)CC)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1c[nH]cn1)C(=O)N1CCC[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)O"
